course: dictionary
lesson: 7998
title: Hidden meanings in formal words
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Formal words are often long and sound important. A redeployment is when people, soldiers, workers or equipment are moved to a different place or job where they are needed more. The clue is in the sentence: «The redeployment of the troops took three days.» Troops were moved from one place to another, and the move took time.
explain: Some formal words hide what really happens. «"Tactical redeployment" is military jargon for a retreat.» Jargon is special language used by a certain group. A retreat means an army moving back, often because it is in danger, so the fancy phrase makes the move sound calmer and more planned.
explain: Businesses can use the same trick. A redeployment of workers sounds gentler than saying people have lost their jobs, although it can mean they are moved to new work instead. Ask yourself: what is this word really saying, and who is it trying to make sound good?
fact: Redeployment means moving people, soldiers, workers or equipment to a different place or job where they are needed more.
fact: The verb is redeploy, which means to move someone or something to a new place or job.
fact: Jargon is special or technical language used by a particular group, such as the army or a business.
fact: «Tactical redeployment» is military jargon for a retreat, so the phrase can hide a move backwards.
fact: Formal words can make a serious change sound calm; check what the words are really doing.
exercise: «The council made a redeployment of its staff to the new library.» What does redeployment mean? | *Moving workers to a different job where they are needed more | Buying new computers for the children | Closing the library for ever | Asking the staff to sing
exercise: The phrase «tactical redeployment» is military jargon. What plain word does it hide? | A new uniform | A victory parade | A birthday | *A retreat
exercise: Which is the verb form of redeployment? | Reploy | *Redeploy | Ploy | Redeem
test: «The company announced a redeployment of workers instead of closing the factory.» What is the best meaning here? | *Workers are moved to new work | The workers are given a long holiday | Workers are sent home for ever | The factory is made bigger
test: Why might a company call a job move a redeployment? | To hide that it is a holiday | Because the word is only used by children | Because it is a shorter word than move | *To sound softer and more formal
exam: Which sentence uses redeployment correctly? | Redeployment is a dog that barks at the postman. | I had a redeployment of cheese sandwiches for lunch. | *The police were given a redeployment to the harbour, where they were needed more. | The redeployment of the sky was very yellow today.
